# Keep the ETL service's build context small — this image only needs to run
# Python extractors, not the frontend or local data (data/ lives on a Railway
# volume, never baked into the image; see Dockerfile).
.git/
data/
# ...and NOTHING under data/ can be re-included, whatever this file says.
#
# `railway up` uploads the build context AFTER applying .gitignore, and
# .gitignore:57 is a bare `data` -- which, having no slash, matches a directory
# of that name at any depth. So every path under `data/` is stripped before
# Docker ever reads this file, and a `!data/...` negation here cannot put it
# back. `!data/guard_registry.json` was added on 2026-08-17 on the belief that
# it could; it is inert, and the registry is still absent from the image.
# Verify in one line: `git check-ignore -v data/guard_registry.json`.
#
# The rule that follows from that, and the reason `reference/` sits at the repo
# root rather than under `data/`:
#
#     data/ is the CORPUS -- generated, gitignored, and mounted from a Railway
#     volume at /app/data. reference/ is committed INPUT that code reads, and it
#     is carried by the image like the code that reads it.
#
# A committed input placed under data/ reaches the container only if a human
# copies it onto the volume, which nothing in this repo does and nothing
# records. That is how the weekly ETL died on 2026-08-16: wsCZ added
# `data/reference/activity_codebook.json` on 2026-08-10, `classify_taxonomy.py`
# reads it at module level, and the first cron run on the image built from that
# commit raised FileNotFoundError before its first statement -- rc=1, six later
# steps and all four guards skipped. See project-mgmt/findings/wsHU_etl_stopped/.
#
# `ETL/schema/check_cron_image_inputs.py` now fails the build on the next one.
#
# The registry is the last committed input still under data/, and the line below
# is kept only so it starts working the day .gitignore:57 stops matching it. The
# real fix is the same one applied to reference/: move it out. Not done here --
# 60-odd readers across the check suite, and none of them is the ETL.
!data/guard_registry.json
website/frontend/node_modules/
website/frontend/.next/
website/frontend/out/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store

# Secrets must never be baked into an image. `COPY . .` would otherwise carry
# .env.local into every layer of a registry image, and the file holds the
# Anthropic, OpenAI, Voyage and Postgres credentials. Railway injects the real
# environment at runtime; nothing in the image should need this file.
.env
.env.*
# Worktrees carry a `.git` FILE rather than a directory, which the `.git/`
# pattern above does not match, and a dangling symlink inside one fails the
# build with an unhelpful COPY error.
.git
.claude/

# A database backup must no more enter an image than a credential does. This
# directory is 123 MB of verified Postgres dumps (ETL/lib/backup_postgres.py),
# untracked and, until now, matched by nothing here -- so `COPY . .` would have
# baked it into every layer, more than doubling a 77 MB build context and
# putting a full copy of the corpus in a registry. Railway mounts the real data
# on a volume; nothing in this image should ever read a backup.
postgres-backup/

# corpus-ledger/ is the committed text copy of the authored instrument corpus.
# The ETL image never reads it (restore is a checkout operation), and it grows
# with every export -- 11 MB today. Keep it out of the build context.
corpus-ledger/
